{SECT 0 {EXCHG {PARA 210 "" 0 "" {TEXT 227 22 "Lecture 2 Flow Control
" }}}{SECT 0 {PARA 208 "" 0 "" {TEXT 226 15 "Shift + Return " }{TEXT 
229 2 "vs" }{TEXT 226 11 " the Return" }{TEXT 268 0 "" }}{EXCHG {PARA 
209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 66 "If the cursor is on the same line as a Maple
 command, hitting the " }{TEXT 231 6 "Return" }{TEXT 228 52 " key will
 cause the command to be executed. Hitting " }{TEXT 231 14 "Shift + Re
turn" }{TEXT 228 16 " (hold down the " }{TEXT 231 5 "Shift" }{TEXT 
228 17 " key and then hit" }{TEXT 231 7 " Return" }{TEXT 228 107 ") wi
ll move the cursor to the next line without executing the command and \+
without introducing a new prompt " }{TEXT 231 1 ">" }{TEXT 228 4 ".  \+
\n" }{TEXT 269 0 "" }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 234 "Sometimes you d
on't want Maple to execute a sequence of commands till you have typed \+
in several on different lines. To do this one needs to know how to go \+
to another output line without getting a new prompt, that is, how to s
uppress  " }{TEXT 231 5 ">.   " }{TEXT 228 21 "In such cases use of " 
}{TEXT 231 14 "Shift + Return" }{TEXT 228 16 " (hold down the " }{TEXT
 231 5 "Shift" }{TEXT 228 12 " key and hit" }{TEXT 231 7 " Return" }
{TEXT 228 24 ") will accomplish this. " }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 
0 "" }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 126 "Before continuing make sure yo
u can type in the following in the space following without getting mor
e than the initial prompt " }{TEXT 231 1 ">" }{TEXT 228 1 "." }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 22 "for i from 1 to 3 do \n"
 }{MPLTEXT 1 230 18 "  print(\"Hello\");\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 7 "end do:
" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 35 "To get the indentation on \+
the line " }{TEXT 230 16 "print(\"Hello\"); " }{TEXT 228 62 "you will \+
have to use the space bar to insert the extra spaces." }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
269 0 "" }}}}{SECT 0 {PARA 208 "" 0 "" {TEXT 226 25 "for . . . do . . \+
. end do" }}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 233 5 "Loops" }{TEXT 228 4 
" or " }{TEXT 233 8 "do loops" }{TEXT 228 203 " provide a way to repea
t operations a specified number of times. They are best understood by \+
examples.  The example in the previous section showed a way to print H
ello three times. Execute the following:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "
" {MPLTEXT 1 230 22 "for i from 1 to 4 do \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 13 "  i,
i^2,i^3;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 7 "end do;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 230 22 "for x from -2 to 2 do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 20 "  x, e
valf(sin(x));\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 7 "end do;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 
"" {TEXT 228 235 "Now we illustrate a common technique for adding a sp
ecified number of terms. There is nothing special about SUM as a varia
ble. We could use any (unprotected) name for this variable. We will fi
nd the sum of the integers 1 through 10.   " }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT
 269 0 "" }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 95 "We start by setting the va
riable SUM to 0. Examine the output carefully to see what's going on."
 }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 8 "SUM:=0;\n" }{MPLTEXT 
1 230 22 "for i from 1 to 5 do \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 16 " SUM:=SUM + i; \+
\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 7 "end do;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 
53 "Note the value of i after the loop has been executed:" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 2 "i;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 228 54 "See the value of SUM after the loop has been executed:" 
}}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 4 "SUM;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 
209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 62 "To suppress the intermediate steps we may pl
ace a colon after " }{TEXT 231 6 "end do" }{TEXT 228 35 " instead of a
 semi-colon and place " }{TEXT 231 4 "SUM;" }{TEXT 228 7 " after " }
{TEXT 231 8 "end do: " }{TEXT 228 77 "This way we just get the result \+
desired with a minimum of unnecessary output." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> 
" 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 8 "SUM:=0:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 22 "for i from 1 t
o 5 do \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 17 " SUM:= SUM + i; \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 8 "
end do:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 4 "SUM;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
228 31 "For another example we compute " }{XPPEDIT 18 0 "Typesetting:-
mrow(Typesetting:-mi(\"\"), Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting:-munderover(
Typesetting:-mo(\"&Sum;\", foreground = \"[144,144,144]\", mathvariant
 = \"normal\", Typesetting:-msemantics = \"inert\", fence = \"false\",
 separator = \"false\", stretchy = \"true\", symmetric = \"false\", la
rgeop = \"true\", movablelimits = \"true\", accent = \"false\", lspace
 = \"0.0em\", rspace = \"0.1666667em\"), Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting
:-mi(\"k\", italic = \"true\", mathvariant = \"italic\"), Typesetting:
-mo(\"=\", mathvariant = \"normal\", fence = \"false\", separator = \"
false\", stretchy = \"false\", symmetric = \"false\", largeop = \"fals
e\", movablelimits = \"false\", accent = \"false\", lspace = \"0.27777
78em\", rspace = \"0.2777778em\"), Typesetting:-mn(\"1\", mathvariant \+
= \"normal\")), Typesetting:-mn(\"100\", mathvariant = \"normal\"), ac
cent = \"false\", accentunder = \"false\"), Typesetting:-mi(\"\"), Typ
esetting:-mspace(height = \"0.0ex\", width = \"5.0\", depth = \"0.0ex
\", linebreak = \"auto\"), Typesetting:-mfrac(Typesetting:-mrow(Typese
tting:-mn(\"1\", mathvariant = \"normal\")), Typesetting:-mrow(Typeset
ting:-mi(\"k\", italic = \"true\", mathvariant = \"italic\")), linethi
ckness = \"1\", denomalign = \"center\", numalign = \"center\", bevell
ed = \"false\")), Typesetting:-mi(\"\"));" "-I%mrowG6#/I+modulenameG6
\"I,TypesettingGI(_syslibGF'6%-I#miGF$6#Q!F'-F#6&-I+munderoverGF$6'-I#
moGF$6/Q&&Sum;F'/%+foregroundGQ.[144,144,144]F'/%,mathvariantGQ'normal
F'/I+msemanticsGF$Q&inertF'/%&fenceGQ&falseF'/%*separatorGFC/%)stretch
yGQ%trueF'/%*symmetricGFC/%(largeopGFH/%.movablelimitsGFH/%'accentGFC/
%'lspaceGQ&0.0emF'/%'rspaceGQ,0.1666667emF'-F#6%-F,6%Q\"kF'/%'italicGF
H/F<Q'italicF'-F56-Q\"=F'F;FAFD/FGFCFI/FLFC/FNFCFO/FRQ,0.2777778emF'/F
UFao-I#mnGF$6$Q\"1F'F;-Fdo6$Q$100F'F;FO/%,accentunderGFCF+-I'mspaceGF$
6&/%'heightGQ&0.0exF'/%&widthGQ$5.0F'/%&depthGFap/%*linebreakGQ%autoF'
-I&mfracGF$6(-F#6#Fco-F#6#FY/%.linethicknessGFfo/%+denomalignGQ'center
F'/%)numalignGFeq/%)bevelledGFCF+" }{TEXT 228 33 " : This time we use \+
the variable " }{TEXT 231 5 "total" }{TEXT 228 60 " to keep track of t
he sum. Of course, any variable would do." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 
"" {MPLTEXT 1 230 10 "total:=0:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 24 "for k from 1 to
 100 do \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 20 " total:=total+1/k; \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 
230 8 "end do: " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 6 "total
;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 49 "If we want to see how big
 this is we apply evalf:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 
230 13 "evalf(total);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }}}}{SECT 0 {PARA 208 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 226 26 "if . . . then . . . end if" }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
269 0 "" }}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 31 "Before giving an exa
mple of an " }{TEXT 231 22 "if ... then ... end if" }{TEXT 228 26 " st
atement we discuss the " }{TEXT 231 3 "mod" }{TEXT 228 23 " procedure.
 The command" }{TEXT 231 9 " n mod d " }{TEXT 228 16 "or, equivalently
" }{TEXT 231 12 ", modp(n,d) " }{TEXT 228 67 "gives the remainder when
 the integer n is divided by the integer d:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 
0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 24 "12 mod 10 = modp(12,10);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 
"> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 22 "12 mod 2 = modp(12,2);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 
209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 22 "12 mod 7 = modp(12,7);" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 13 "Note that if " }{TEXT 231 11 "n mod d \+
= 0" }{TEXT 228 13 " then d is a " }{TEXT 231 6 "factor" }{TEXT 228 4 
" or " }{TEXT 231 7 "divisor" }{TEXT 228 38 " of n. Let's find all div
isors of 28. " }{TEXT 232 62 "Notice the way we indent here, so it is \+
easier to identify the" }{TEXT 228 1 " " }{TEXT 234 9 "do....end" }
{TEXT 232 1 " " }{TEXT 234 2 "do" }{TEXT 232 6 "  and " }{TEXT 234 13 
"if ... end if" }{TEXT 232 15 " constructions." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 213 ">
 " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 7 "n:=28;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 22 "for d from 1 t
o n do \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 23 "  if n mod d = 0 then \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 
230 15 "    print(d); \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 10 "  end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 
1 230 7 "end do:" }{MPLTEXT 1 228 1 "\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 228 58 "Change the value of n above and re-execute the procedure
. " }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 
33 "Here's another example using the " }{TEXT 231 22 "if ... then ... \+
end if" }{TEXT 228 47 " construction. Read it carefully. You should be
" }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 29 "able to see what's happening." }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 22 "for k from 1 to 50 do\n"
 }{MPLTEXT 1 230 23 "  a:=3*k^2 + 10*k + 1:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 18 "  r
:= modp(a,31);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 20 "  if r  <  3 then  \n" }{MPLTEXT
 1 230 17 "    print(k,r); \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 10 "  end if;\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 230 7 "end do:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 55 "N
ote that the same task can be accomplished as follows:" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 22 "for k from 1 to 50 do\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 230 32 "  r:=modp(3*k^2 + 10*k + 1,31);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 
38 "  if r  <  3 then print(k,r); end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 7 "end do
:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0
 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }}}}{SECT 0 {PARA 208 "" 0 "" {TEXT 226 42 "The set
 of positive divisors of an integer" }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 0 "
" }}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 28 "Now we show how to form the
 " }{TEXT 233 3 "set" }{TEXT 228 113 " of all positive divisors of 28.
 A student of number theory will easily see how to improve this little
 program.  " }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 228 4 "The " }{TEXT 231 10 "printlevel" }{TEXT 228 75 " is 1 by \+
default. Changing it to 2 allows us to see what's happening below:" }}
}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 14 "printlevel:=2:" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 214 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 7 "n:=28:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 
230 7 "S:=\{\}; " }{MPLTEXT 1 228 49 "Start with the empty set which i
s denoted by \{ \}." }{MPLTEXT 1 230 1 "\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 21 "for d \+
from 1 to n do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 22 " if n mod d = 0 then \n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 230 21 "   S:= S union \{d\}; \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 9 " end i
f;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 7 "end do;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
228 39 "Let's see what values d and S have now." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "
> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 4 "d,S;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
269 0 "" }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 260 "Now move the cursor back t
o printlevel:=2; , change the 2 to a 1 and re-execute all the above co
mmands. Note that the intermediate steps are now suppressed. Note that
 you may also change the assignment n:=28; to n:=1284; or anything you
 please and re-execute. " }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }}{PARA 
209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 61 "A simpler way to handle the problem in gener
al is to build a " }{TEXT 233 9 "procedure" }{TEXT 228 89 " to find th
e set of all positive divisors  of any integer n. We will call this pr
ocedure " }{TEXT 231 8 "Divisors" }{TEXT 228 120 ". Examine this proce
dure closely. Note the use of return on the next to last line of the d
efinition of this procedure.  " }{TEXT 232 11 "DO NOT USE " }{TEXT 
234 5 "print" }{TEXT 232 13 " in place of " }{TEXT 234 6 "return" }
{TEXT 232 40 ".  We will explain the difference later." }}{PARA 0 "" 0
 "" {TEXT 270 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 18 "Di
visors:=proc(n)\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 7 "S:=\{\}:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 21 "
for d from 1 to n do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 22 " if n mod d = 0 then \n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 230 20 "   S:=S union \{d\}; \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 9 " end if
;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 8 "end do;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 10 "return S;\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 230 9 "end proc;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 212 "" 0 "" {TEXT 271 0 "
" }}{PARA 212 "" 0 "" {TEXT 213 249 "Notice the warning messages when \+
you execute the statement defining the above procedure. Also notice th
at Maple reprints the procedure if you end the definition with a semi-
colon . We remedy both of these problems in the following corrected ve
rsion." }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 
"" {MPLTEXT 1 230 18 "Divisors:=proc(n)\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 11 "local S
,d;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 7 "S:=\{\}:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 21 "for d from 1
 to n do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 45 " if n mod d = 0 then S:=S union \{d\};
 end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 8 "end do;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 10 "return S
;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 10 "end proc:\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 234 10 "Important:" }{TEXT 232 190 " Make it a habit to end a pr
ocedure with a colon instead of a semi-colon to save output--especiall
y when printing your worksheet. Also ALWAYS declare all variables used
 in a procedure to be " }{TEXT 234 5 "local" }{TEXT 232 61 " as we do \+
above, unless there is a good reason not to do so. " }{TEXT 228 45 "La
ter we will discuss the difference between " }{TEXT 231 5 "local" }
{TEXT 228 5 " and " }{TEXT 231 6 "global" }{TEXT 228 12 " variables.\n
" }{TEXT 228 1 "\n" }{TEXT 228 29 "Now let's test the procedure " }
{TEXT 231 8 "Divisors" }{TEXT 228 186 ": Try some things first that we
 know the answer to in order to make some check on the correctness. Of
 course, this doesn't prove it works always but often such checks will
 catch errors. " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 12 "Divi
sors(1);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 12 "Divisors(2)
;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 12 "Divisors(6);" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 14 "Divisors(126);" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 16 "Divisors(12345);" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }}}}{SECT 0 {PARA 208 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 226 28 "More examples of procedures " }}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 ""
 {TEXT 228 49 "Let's look again at how to compute a sum such as " }
