yFunction;F'F8F;F>FgnFCFhnFinFIFK/FOFM-I(mfencedGF$6$-F#6#FSF8F+F+" }
{TEXT 228 114 "  where a and b are any integers and f is any function.
 We will call the procedure S. Note that it requires three " }{TEXT 
233 9 "arguments" }{TEXT 228 13 ": f, a and b:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 ">
 " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 15 "S:=proc(f,a,b)\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 16 "local
 i, Total;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 10 "Total:=0;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 21 "for
 i from a to b do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 23 "  Total:=Total + f(i);\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 230 8 "end do;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 14 "return Total;\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 230 10 "end proc:\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 
37 "Some applications of our procedure S:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 
"" {MPLTEXT 1 230 11 "S(sin,1,5);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 230 16 "S(x->x^2, 1, 6);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 230 10 "S(f,-3,5);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 
0 "" }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 70 "Next we make a procedure we cal
l H which has the property that H(n) = " }{XPPEDIT 18 0 "Typesetting:-
mrow(Typesetting:-mi(\"&infin;\", italic = \"false\", mathvariant = \"
normal\"));" "-I%mrowG6#/I+modulenameG6\"I,TypesettingGI(_syslibGF'6#-
I#miGF$6%Q(&infin;F'/%'italicGQ&falseF'/%,mathvariantGQ'normalF'" }
{TEXT 228 49 "  if  n is greater than 100 and H(n) =  0 if not." }}
{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 230 11 "H:=proc(n)\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 17 "if n > 100 then \+
\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 20 "  return infinity; \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 4 "fi;
\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 10 "return 0;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 4 "end:" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 
66 "Note that if n > 100 is true then H immediately returns the value 
" }{XPPEDIT 18 0 "Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting:-mi(\"&infin;\", itali
c = \"false\", mathvariant = \"normal\"));" "-I%mrowG6#/I+modulenameG6
\"I,TypesettingGI(_syslibGF'6#-I#miGF$6%Q(&infin;F'/%'italicGQ&falseF'
/%,mathvariantGQ'normalF'" }{TEXT 228 149 "  and therefore does not pr
oceed to the next statement. On the other hand, if n > 100 is false th
en Maple skips down to the last line and returns 0. " }}{PARA 209 "" 0
 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 7 "H(2
21);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 6 "H(11);" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 98 "As another example, the factori
al fuction on the non-negative integers can be achieved as follows " }
}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 23 "Factorialproc:=proc(n)
\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 14 "local Fac, i:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 8 "Fac:=1:\n"
 }{MPLTEXT 1 230 27 "if n=0 then Fac:=1 end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 13 
"if n>0 then \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 21 "for i from 1 to n do\n" }{MPLTEXT
 1 230 15 "   Fac:=i*Fac:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 11 "   end do \n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 230 11 "   end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 11 "return Fac\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 230 10 "end proc; " }}{PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 
52 "Factorialproc(0),Factorialproc(5),Factorialproc(10);" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }}}}{SECT 0 {PARA 208 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
226 34 "Making a program into a procedure." }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
228 134 "When constructing a procedure it is a good idea to test the c
ommands individually first so you can be sure what is happening. One m
ay " }{TEXT 231 3 "cut" }{TEXT 228 2 ", " }{TEXT 231 4 "copy" }{TEXT 
228 5 " and " }{TEXT 231 5 "paste" }{TEXT 228 187 " text and Maple inp
ut and output pretty much at will.  This is useful when constructing a
 procedure from some program that you have already tested. You may ill
ustrate this by copying the " }{TEXT 232 7 "program" }{TEXT 228 28 " b
elow and making it into a " }{TEXT 232 9 "procedure" }{TEXT 228 19 " a
s we did above.  " }{TEXT 232 204 "To perform calculations with Maple \+
one need not always write a  procedure. Writing a procedure is really \+
only necessary if you need to use some program repeatedly with differe
nt values of the parameters. " }}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 
1 230 7 "n:=4: \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 10 "Total:=0:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 
21 "for i from 1 to n do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 20 "  Total:=Total+i^2:\n"
 }{MPLTEXT 1 230 8 "end do:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 6 "Total;" }}{PARA 11 "
" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "6#\"#I" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 
230 7 "n:=10:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 18 "Factorialloop:=1:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 
230 21 "for i from 1 to n do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 34 "  Factorialloop:=F
actorialloop*i:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 8 "end do:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 14 "F
actorialloop;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }}}}{SECT 0 
{PARA 208 "" 0 "" {TEXT 226 39 "Order of execution of commands in Mapl
e" }}{PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT
 228 347 "In a single execution group Maple executes the commands in o
rder from top to bottom. However, if you go back and change some compu
tation in a different execution group then it does not change the foll
owing results unless the entire worksheet is executed once more. Here \+
is a simple example. First execute the following commands in the order
 given." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 5 "x:=2;" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 5 "y:=3;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 
209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 7 "z:=x*y;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 ""
 {MPLTEXT 1 230 2 "z;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 190 "Now \+
go back and change the 2 in the line x:=2 to a 5 and execute that comm
and. Notice that the value of z has not changed.  To change the value \+
of z to 15 we must re-execute the line z:=x*y." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 ">
 " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 2 "z;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 228 
227 "Of course, we would not have this problem if we put all the three
 commands in a single execution group as we do below. But after changi
ng the 2 to a 5 in x:=2; we must execute the execution group for the c
hanges to take effect." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 
6 "x:=2:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 6 "y:=3:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 230 7 "z:=x*y:" }}
}{EXCHG {PARA 209 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 230 2 "z;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 212 
"" 0 "" {TEXT 213 176 "The moral of this is that when executing the li
nes in a Maple worksheet it is important which order they are executed
 in rather than the order in which they appear on the page." }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 209 "" 0 "" {TEXT 269 0 "" }{TEXT 214 0 "" }}}}{SECT 0 
{PARA 3 "" 0 "" {TEXT 272 9 "Exercises" }}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT
 270 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 235 10 "Problem 1." }{TEXT 270 3 "  \+
\n" }{TEXT 270 58 "(a) Write a program (not necessarily a procedure) u
sing a " }{TEXT 236 7 "do loop" }{TEXT 270 20 " to compute the sum " }
{XPPEDIT 18 0 "Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting:-mi(\"\"), Typesetting:-m
row(Typesetting:-munderover(Typesetting:-mo(\"&Sum;\", mathvariant = \+
